{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% block content %}
<p>D&amp;D lives on dice. But LLMs are terrible at randomness: they are trained to produce
the <em>likely</em> next token, and they have a strong bias toward dramatic, convenient
outcomes. Ask one to "roll a d20" and you'll see a lot of 17s.</p>

<p>So we split the job:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The <strong>model</strong> decides <em>when</em> to roll, <em>what</em> dice, and what
      counts as success.</li>
  <li>The <strong>engine</strong> does the actual rolling with Python's <code>random</code>.</li>
  <li>The model must narrate the result honestly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The tool</h2>
{{ snippet("tools.py", "roll_tool") }}
<p>The <code>rng</code> lives in the <code>TurnLog</code>, so tests can pass a seeded
<code>random.Random(42)</code> and get reproducible rolls.</p>

